md`# SoundScript specification

This page defines SoundScript: what a cell is, what the checker accepts and rejects, and what the notebook does with cells when they run and when they are edited. SoundScript is a checked subset of TypeScript for notebook cells: a cell is a function, and its parameters are the cells it reads. The [tutorial](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-tutorial")}) is the place to start. Behaviour the code has that this page does not state is not promised; the known cases are listed in the last section.`
// %%
md`Each statement has a number, such as **S3.2**, which stays the same when statements are added. A section has its statements, then cells written in SoundScript that the statements describe, then a table of sources the checker must reject with the rule each must give, then tests. A test is a cell named after the statement it checks: \`test_spec_S3_2\` checks S3.2. The coverage table counts, for each section, its statements, how many have a test and how many tests pass. The tests run when the page loads. The table is drawn again as each test finishes and when a cell of this page is added, edited or deleted; a test that has not finished is counted as running.`
// %%
md`**Related work.** The static rules for local writes (section 4) borrow the shape of reference immutability with uniqueness: Gordon, Parkinson, Parsons, Bromfield and Duffy, *Uniqueness and Reference Immutability for Safe Parallelism*, OOPSLA 2012 ([extended version, MSR-TR-2012-79](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/msr-tr-2012-79.pdf)). They are not sound alone: the guarantee rests on the freeze at run time (S4.6).

- That system has four permissions on a reference: writable, readable, immutable and isolated. In SoundScript \`Mut<T>\` is writable, at its first level only (S4.7); the paper's writable reaches every level. Inside its cell, a plain \`T\` that is another name for a \`Mut\` (S4.4) is readable in the paper's sense: it cannot write, and another name can. Once published, a plain \`T\` is immutable. There is no isolated permission.
- Its recovery rule says: when everything a block reads is immutable or isolated, a writable value the block produces has no alias outside the block, so it may be treated as isolated and then as immutable, with no tracking of aliases inside the block. A cell reads only frozen values, which is the premise of that rule.
- The rule needs a language with no mutable global state, and it says nothing about code that runs after the block has given its value. The paper's formal language has no closures, arrays or exceptions, and it models parallelism as two blocks that both end, with no task that is joined later. In SoundScript the code that runs later is a kept function, a generator after a \`yield\` and an async function after an \`await\`; S4.5, S4.6 and S4.8 cover them.
- The paper describes no run-time check. SoundScript freezes each value when it is published (S4.6), and that freeze is what stops a write the static rules miss.`
// %%
spec_coverage_view = {
  const rows = spec_coverage.rows.map((r) => r.statements === 0
    ? htl.html`<tr><td>${r.section}</td><td>${r.title}</td><td colspan="4">no statements yet</td></tr>`
    : htl.html`<tr><td>${r.section}</td><td>${r.title}</td><td>${r.statements}</td><td>${r.tested}</td><td>${r.passing}/${r.tests}${r.running ? ` (${r.running} running)` : ""}</td><td>${r.untested.join(", ")}</td></tr>`);
  const unknown = spec_coverage.unknown.length ? htl.html`<p>Tests naming a statement that does not exist: ${spec_coverage.unknown.join(", ")}</p>` : "";
  return htl.html`<table><thead><tr><th>§</th><th>section</th><th>statements</th><th>with a test</th><th>tests passing</th><th>statements with no test</th></tr></thead><tbody>${rows}</tbody></table>${unknown}`;
}
// %%
md`## 1. Cells and the top level

A module is a list of cells. A cell is a named function at the top level. The rest of the top level is types.`
// %%
md`**S1.1** A cell is a top-level \`function\`, \`function*\`, \`async function\` or \`async function*\` declaration, or one declarator of a top-level \`const\` or \`let\` whose initializer is an arrow or function expression. The declared name is the cell's name.`
// %%
md`**S1.2** \`export\` before a cell or a type declaration is accepted and changes nothing.`
// %%
md`**S1.3** The top level may also hold \`type\` and \`interface\` declarations and \`import type\`. They are not cells.`
// %%
md`**S1.4** Any other top-level statement is \`[module-level]\`, except \`var\`, \`class\`, \`enum\` and \`namespace\`, which are \`[no-var]\`, \`[no-class]\`, \`[no-enum]\` and \`[no-namespace]\` (section 3).`
// %%
md`**S1.5** A top-level \`const\` or \`let\` that is not a function is \`[cell-not-function]\`. That includes a declaration with no initializer and one that destructures.`
// %%
md`**S1.6** A name declared by more than one cell belongs to the first. Each later cell with that name is \`[duplicate-cell]\` ("a is already defined at line 1") and is reported as \`a (2)\`, \`a (3)\` and so on. The first is checked as usual, and a cell that takes the name reads the first. In a notebook module, an edit that gives a cell the name of another cell is stored under \`a (2)\` and does not run. The compiled variable of \`a (2)\` is named \`$ss$2$a\`, and a type declaration \`type X\` is the variable \`$ss$type$X\`, so that each has a legal Observable JavaScript name.`
// %%
md`**S1.7** A cell with the name of a shell cell is \`[duplicate-cell]\`. A shell cell is an unchecked cell the checker is given beside the checked ones (section 9); a notebook module gives it none.`
// %%
md`**S1.8** One \`const\` or \`let\` statement may declare several cells: \`const a = () => 1, b = () => 2;\` is two cells.`
// %%
md`**S1.9** A cell that takes itself, or takes a cell that through its own inputs takes it back, is \`[cycle]\` ("dependency cycle a -> b -> a"). Every cell on the cycle is rejected.`
// %%
md`**S1.10** A top-level \`type\` or \`interface\` named as one of the checker's own types, \`Array ReadonlyArray Record Js ValueOf String Number Boolean RegExp RegExpMatchArray Map Set Error Promise ArrayBuffer URL URLSearchParams Float32Array Float64Array Int8Array Uint8Array Uint8ClampedArray Int16Array Uint16Array Int32Array Uint32Array Mut Borrowed State StateCell Action Events Listener Generator Iterable ArrayLike Clock Random Fetch FetchResponse Log Invalidation Compile Pool Cast Elem Child PointerData CommonProps ButtonProps InputProps CanvasProps IntrinsicElements\`, is \`[builtin-type-name]\`. It is not loaded: the cells of the module read the checker's type of that name. Since 2026-11-23 a type alias that is defined by itself with no object, array, tuple or function type between, through names, unions, intersections, parentheses and generic aliases that give back an argument (\`type Key = Key\`, \`type A = B | number; type B = A\`), is \`[type-cycle]\` and is not loaded either; a use of such an alias adds no error of its own, so the program has the one \`[type-cycle]\` per alias (since 2026-11-23, a9; before, each use was also \`[unknown-type]\`); \`type Tree = { readonly kids: readonly Tree[] }\` is accepted.`
// %%
md`The cells of this section, written in SoundScript. \`s1_exported\` keeps its \`export\` (S1.2), and the editor marks it "export is not needed".`
// %%
//ss: ./spec.ts
// %%
md`Rejected sources. Each is checked when the page loads; the fourth column is what the checker reports and the last whether that is the rule given.`
// %%
spec_rejects_1 = [
  { statement: "S1.4", rule: "module-level", source: "for (;;) {}\nlet a = () => 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.4", rule: "module-level", source: "1 + 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.4", rule: "no-class", source: "export class A {}\nconst a = () => 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.4", rule: "no-enum", source: "export enum E { a }\nconst a = () => 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.5", rule: "cell-not-function", source: "let a = () => 1;\nlet x = 42;" },
  { statement: "S1.5", rule: "cell-not-function", source: "let x;" },
  { statement: "S1.5", rule: "cell-not-function", source: "const { a } = { a: () => 1 };" },
  { statement: "S1.6", rule: "duplicate-cell", source: "const a = () => 1;\nconst a = () => 2;" },
  { statement: "S1.7", rule: "duplicate-cell", source: "let a = () => 1;", shell: "export let a = () => 2;" },
  { statement: "S1.9", rule: "cycle", source: "const a = (a: number) => a + 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.9", rule: "cycle", source: "const a = (b: number) => b + 1;\nconst b = (a: number) => a + 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "builtin-type-name", source: "type Listener = { readonly n: number };" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "builtin-type-name", source: "interface Array<T> { readonly length: number; push(x: T): number }" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "builtin-type-name", source: "type Pool = { readonly size: number };\nconst a = (pool: Pool) => pool.size;" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "type-cycle", source: "type Key = Key;" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "type-cycle", source: "type A = B | number;\ntype B = A;" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "type-cycle", source: "type Box<T> = T;\ntype K = Box<K>;" }
]
// %%
spec_rejects_1_view = spec_rejectsTable(spec_rejects_1)
// %%
spec_rejects = [...spec_rejects_1, ...spec_rejects_2, ...spec_rejects_3, ...spec_rejects_4, ...spec_rejects_5, ...spec_rejects_6, ...spec_rejects_7, ...spec_rejects_9, ...spec_rejects_10, ...spec_rejects_11]
// %%
spec_rulesOf = (c) => {
  // the rule ids the checker reports for a reject case, sorted and without repeats. outside: the cells in
  // other languages the case's module has (section 11)
  const r = soundscript.checkSources({ cells: c.source, shell: c.shell, tsx: true, outside: c.outside });
  return [...new Set([...r.module, ...r.cells.flatMap((x) => x.errors)].map((e) => e.rule))].sort();
}
// %%
spec_rejectsTable = (cases) => htl.html`<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>source</th><th>rule</th><th>checker</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody>${cases.map((c) => {
  const got = spec_rulesOf(c);
  const same = got.length === 1 && got[0] === c.rule;
  const notes = [c.shell ? "// shell: " + c.shell : "", c.outside ? "// other languages: " + (c.outside.map((o) => (o.view ? "viewof " : "") + o.name).join(", ") || "none") : ""].filter(Boolean);
  return htl.html`<tr><td>${c.statement}</td><td><pre style="margin:0">${[c.source, ...notes].join("\n")}</pre></td><td>[${c.rule}]</td><td>${got.map((r) => `[${r}]`).join(" ")}</td><td>${same ? "same" : "DIFFERENT"}</td></tr>`;
})}</tbody></table>`
// %%
spec_rejectsFor = (statement) => {
  // every reject case of a statement gives exactly its rule
  const cases = spec_rejects.filter((c) => c.statement === statement);
  if (!cases.length) throw new Error(`${statement} has no reject case`);
  for (const c of cases) {
    const got = spec_rulesOf(c);
    if (got.length !== 1 || got[0] !== c.rule) throw new Error(`${JSON.stringify(c.source)} gave ${JSON.stringify(got)}, expected exactly [${c.rule}]`);
  }
  return `${cases.length} source(s) give exactly [${[...new Set(cases.map((c) => c.rule))].join(", ")}]`;
}
// %%
// sources a deleted ownership rule rejected that are accepted since the 2026-11-22 language change (S4): a cell's value
// is frozen when it leaves the cell, so what another cell holds cannot change; and (2026-11-23, e1) writes on a receiver
// made in the same expression (S4.3). was: the rule that rejected it
spec_accepts = [
  { statement: "S3.1", was: "member-write", source: "const w = () => {\n  const l = { a: 1 };\n  l.a = 2;\n  return l;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.11", was: "let-capture", source: "const passed = () => {\n  let n = 0;\n  [1, 2].forEach((x) => {\n    n += x;\n  });\n  return n;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.18", was: "mut-escape", source: "const f = () => (a: number) => {\n  const m = [1];\n  switch (a) {\n    case 1: return m;\n  }\n  return m;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.18", was: "mut-after-freeze", source: "const f = () => (a: number): readonly number[] => {\n  const m = [1];\n  let out: readonly number[] = [];\n  switch (a) {\n    case 1: out = m; break;\n  }\n  m.push(2);\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.18", was: "mut-after-freeze", source: "const f = () => (xs: readonly number[]): readonly number[] => {\n  const m = [1];\n  let out: readonly number[] = [];\n  for (const x of xs) {\n    switch (x) {\n      case 1: out = m; continue;\n    }\n    m.push(x);\n  }\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.18", was: "let-capture", source: "const f = () => (a: number): number => {\n  let n = 0;\n  switch (a) {\n    case 1: return [1].map((x) => { n = x; return x; }).length;\n  }\n  return n;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-bind", source: "const a = () => {\n  let out = [0];\n  out[0] = 1;\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-bind", source: "const a = () => [0];" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-bind", source: "const a = () => [[0]];" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-bind", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = { h: { t: [1] } };\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-field-assign", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = { t: [1] };\n  g.t = [2];\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mut-not-fresh", source: "const bad = () => {\n  const a = [1];\n  const f = a;\n  const b = f;\n  b[0] = 2;\n  return b;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const sum = () => (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((p, q) => p + q, 0);\nconst a = (sum) => {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  const alias = out;\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const sum = () => (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((p, q) => p + q, 0);\nconst a = (sum) => {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  const arr = [out];\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const sum = () => (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((p, q) => p + q, 0);\nconst a = (sum) => {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  sum(out);\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const a = () => {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const b = () => {\n  const out = [0];\n  return { out };\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const g = function* () {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  yield out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = { t: [1] };\n  const t = g.t;\n  return g.t.length;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = { t: [1] };\n  const h = { ...g };\n  return g.t.length;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.5", was: "mut-capture", source: "const a = () => {\n  const out = [0];\n  [1].forEach(() => {\n    out[0] = 1;\n  });\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.6", was: "mut-after-freeze", source: "const a = () => {\n  const out = [0];\n  const r = out;\n  out[0] = 2;\n  return r;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.6", was: "mut-after-freeze", source: "const a = () => {\n  const out = [0];\n  const f = () => {\n    out[0] = 1;\n  };\n  const r = out;\n  f();\n  return r;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.8", was: "mut-view", source: "const a = () => {\n  const t = new Float32Array(4);\n  const v = t.subarray(0, 2);\n  return t;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.8", was: "mut-view", source: "const a = () => {\n  const m = new Map<string, number>();\n  const set = (k: string) => m.set(k, 1);\n  set(\"a\");\n  return m.size;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.9", was: "mut-freeze-param", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = (m: Mut<number[]>) => m;\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.1", was: "borrow-placement", source: "const a = () => {\n  const b = [1];\n  return snapshot(b);\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.1", was: "borrow-placement", source: "const g = function* () {\n  const b = [1];\n  const f = function* () {\n    yield b;\n  };\n  yield 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.2", was: "inject-type", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst plain = (frames: Float32Array) => frames.length;" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => frames;" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => {\n  const f = () => frames.length;\n  return f();\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => [frames];" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => state(frames);" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => {\n  let x = frames;\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = function* (frames) {\n  yield frames;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.4", was: "borrowed-await", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = async (frames) => {\n  await Promise.resolve();\n  return frames.length;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.5", was: "type", source: "const f = () => snapshot([1]);" },
  { statement: "S2.11", was: "type", source: "const f = () => {\n  const g = async () => Promise.resolve(5);\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S2.11", was: "type", source: "const f = async () => {\n  async function g(): Promise<number> {\n    return Promise.resolve(5);\n  }\n  const n: number = await g();\n  return n;\n};" },
  { statement: "S7.1", was: "inject-events", source: "const keys = function* (now: Clock) {\n  yield { key: \"a\", t: now() };\n};\nconst onKey = () => action((e: { readonly key: string; readonly t: number }) => {});\nconst l = (keys: Events<{ readonly key: string; readonly t: number }>, onKey: Action<[{ readonly key: string; readonly t: number }]>) => on(keys, onKey);" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "member-write", source: "const a = () => {\n  const m: { [k: string]: number } = {};\n  m[\"a\"] = 1;\n  return m;\n};" },
  { statement: "S6.20", was: "excess-property", source: "const B = () => (p: { readonly n: number }) => <span>{p.n}</span>;\nconst v = (B: (p: { readonly n: number }) => Elem) => <div>{[1, 2].map((n) => <B key={n} n={n} />)}</div>;" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mutating-method", source: "const xs = () => [3, 1];\nconst b = (xs: readonly number[]) => [...xs].sort();" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mutating-method", source: "const xs = () => [3, 1];\nconst b = (xs: readonly number[]) => xs.slice().reverse();" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "member-write", source: "const xs = () => [3, 1];\nconst b = (xs: readonly number[]) => {\n  const ys = [...xs];\n  [...xs][0] = 9;\n  return ys;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mutating-method", source: "const xs = () => [3, 1];\nconst b = (xs: readonly number[]) => xs.map((x) => x * 2).sort();" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mutating-method", source: "const m = () => new Map<string, number>();\nconst b = (m: Map<string, number>) => new Map(m).set(\"a\", 1).size;" }
]
// %%
spec_acceptsFor = (statement) => {
  const cases = spec_accepts.filter((c) => c.statement === statement);
  if (!cases.length) throw new Error(`${statement} has no accepted case`);
  for (const c of cases) spec_okCells(c.source);
  return `${cases.length} source(s) once rejected by [${[...new Set(cases.map((c) => c.was))].join(", ")}] are accepted`;
}
// %%
spec_okCells = (source) => {
  // the cells of an accepted source, name:status; throws if the source is rejected
  const r = soundscript.check(source);
  const rules = [...r.module, ...r.cells.flatMap((x) => x.errors)].map((e) => e.rule);
  if (rules.length) throw new Error(`${JSON.stringify(source)} gave ${JSON.stringify(rules)}`);
  return r.cells.map((c) => `${c.name}:${c.status}`).join(",");
}
// %%
spec_is = (got, want) => {
  // throws unless got and want are the same JSON
  if (JSON.stringify(got) !== JSON.stringify(want)) throw new Error(`gave ${JSON.stringify(got)}, expected ${JSON.stringify(want)}`);
  return got;
}
// %%
spec_flush = () => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0)).then(() => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0)))
// %%
spec_frames = async (f, n = 600) => {
  // animation frames until f() resolves true, at most n; the last f()
  for (let i = 0; i < n && !(await f()); i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
  return f();
}
// %%
spec_runs = {
  // computations of a variable the runtime started since spec_runs was first asked about it: the pending calls
  // of an observer (runtime-sdk observe), one per computation. observe also calls pending once on attaching, in
  // a microtask it queues then; the first answer waits for it and does not count it.
  const counts = new WeakMap();
  return async (v) => {
    let c = counts.get(v);
    if (!c) {
      c = { n: 0 };
      counts.set(v, c);
      observe(v, { pending: () => c.n++ });
      await null;
      c.n = 0;
    }
    return c.n;
  };
}
// %%
spec_evals = (module, name) => {
  // runs of a cell (spec_runs); a state cell runs in its handle variable
  const v = (n) => [...runtime._variables].find((x) => x._module === module && x._name === n);
  return spec_runs(v(soundscript.handleName(name)) ?? v(name));
}
// %%
spec_editor = async (texts) => {
  // a module of its own that hosts SoundScript (it has ss_rejected), whose cells are applied and edited as the editor does (ss_language.apply);
  // value(n) is the value, or { error } with the message it raises; dispose deletes every variable
  const m = runtime.module();
  m.define(soundscript.INPUTS.rejected, [], () => soundscript.rejected);
  const at = { module: { module: m } };
  for (const t of texts) await ss_language.apply(t, [], at);
  const variable = (n) => m._scope.get(n);
  return {
    module: m,
    at,
    variable,
    edit: (name, text) => ss_language.apply(text, [variable(name)], at),
    states: () => Object.fromEntries([...ss_check(m).states].map(([n, x]) => [n, x.state === "blocked" ? `blocked by ${x.blockedBy.join(",")}` : x.state])),
    value: async (n) => { try { return await m.value(n); } catch (e) { return { error: e.message }; } },
    evals: (n) => spec_runs(variable(soundscript.handleName(n)) ?? variable(n)),
    text: (n) => soundscript.decompileVariable(String(variable(n)._definition)),
    dispose: () => { for (const v of [...runtime._variables]) if (v._module === m) { soundscript.release(v._value); v.delete(); } }
  };
}
// %%
spec_okCellsWith = (source, outside) => {
  // spec_okCells for a module that also has cells in other languages
  const r = soundscript.checkSources({ cells: source, tsx: true, outside });
  const rules = [...r.module, ...r.cells.flatMap((x) => x.errors)].map((e) => e.rule);
  if (rules.length) throw new Error(`${JSON.stringify(source)} gave ${JSON.stringify(rules)}`);
  return r.cells.map((c) => `${c.name}:${c.status}`).join(",");
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_1 = {
  const forms = "const a = () => 1;\nlet b = (a: number) => a + 1;\nconst g = function (a: number) { return a; };\nfunction c(b: number) { return b * 2; }\nfunction* d() { yield 1; }\nasync function e() { return 1; }\nasync function* f() { yield 1; }";
  const got = spec_okCells(forms);
  if (got !== "a:ok,b:ok,g:ok,c:ok,d:ok,e:ok,f:ok") throw new Error(got);
  const values = await Promise.all(["s1_arrow", "s1_let", "s1_declared", "s1_generator", "s1_async"].map((n) => spec_module.value(n)));
  if (JSON.stringify(values) !== "[1,2,4,1,4]") throw new Error(`the live cells gave ${JSON.stringify(values)}`);
  return `7 forms check as cells; the live cells give ${JSON.stringify(values)}`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_2 = {
  const plain = "const a = () => 1;\nfunction c(a: number) { return a; }\ntype T = number;\ninterface I { x: number }";
  const exported = plain.replace(/^/gm, "export ");
  const a = spec_okCells(plain), b = spec_okCells(exported);
  if (a !== b) throw new Error(`${a} without export, ${b} with`);
  if ((await spec_module.value("s1_exported")) !== 5) throw new Error("s1_exported is not 5");
  return `the same cells with and without export (${a}); s1_exported is 5`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_3 = {
  const got = spec_okCells('type T = number;\ninterface I { x: number }\nimport type { X } from "./x";\nconst a = (): T => 1;');
  if (got !== "a:ok") throw new Error(got);
  if (JSON.stringify(await spec_module.value("s1_typed")) !== '{"a":1,"b":2}') throw new Error("s1_typed");
  return "type, interface and import type are accepted and are not cells";
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_4 = {
  const own = [["var a = () => 1;", "no-var"], ["class A {}", "no-class"], ["enum E { A }", "no-enum"], ["namespace N {}", "no-namespace"]];
  for (const [source, rule] of own) {
    const got = spec_rulesOf({ source });
    if (got.length !== 1 || got[0] !== rule) throw new Error(`${source} gave ${JSON.stringify(got)}, expected [${rule}]`);
  }
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S1.4")}; var, class, enum and namespace give their own rules`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_5 = spec_rejectsFor("S1.5")
// %%
test_spec_S1_6 = {
  const r = soundscript.check('const a = () => 1;\nconst b = (a: number) => a + 1;\nconst a = () => "x";\nconst a = () => 3;');
  const got = r.cells.map((c) => `${c.name}:${c.status}:${c.errors.map((e) => e.msg).join("|")}`).join(",");
  if (got !== "a:ok:,b:ok:,a (2):rejected:a is already defined at line 1,a (3):rejected:a is already defined at line 1") throw new Error(got);
  if (r.cells[1].value !== "number") throw new Error(`b reads ${r.cells[1].value}, not the first a`);
  // an edit naming another cell is stored, rejected, under the next free name
  const e = ss_language.check("const s1_arrow = () => 9;", [], { module: { module: spec_module } });
  if (!e.store || e.state !== "rejected" || e.defs.map((d) => soundscript.keyOfVariable(d.name)).join() !== "s1_arrow (2)" || !e.problems.some((p) => p.rule === "duplicate-cell")) throw new Error(`the edit gave store ${e.store}, ${e.state}, ${e.defs?.map((d) => d.name)}, ${JSON.stringify(e.problems)}`);
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S1.6")}; b reads the first a; an edit naming s1_arrow is stored as s1_arrow (2), rejected`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_6_first_deleted = {
  // a module of its own, edited as the editor edits: a second dupa is stored as dupa (2); once the first is
  // deleted, the module is checked again and dupa (2) becomes dupa
  const m = runtime.module();
  m.define(soundscript.INPUTS.rejected, [], () => soundscript.rejected);
  const at = { module: { module: m } };
  const named = () => [...runtime._variables].filter((v) => v._module === m && typeof v._name === "string" && soundscript.keyOfVariable(v._name).startsWith("dupa")).map((v) => soundscript.keyOfVariable(v._name)).sort().join(",");
  const until = async (f) => { for (let i = 0; i < 200 && !f(); i++) await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50)); return f(); };
  try {
    await ss_language.apply("const dupa = () => 1;", [], at);
    await ss_language.apply("const dupa = () => 2;", [], at);
    if (named() !== "dupa,dupa (2)") throw new Error(`after two edits: ${named()}`);
    m._scope.get("dupa").delete();
    if (!(await until(() => named() === "dupa"))) throw new Error(`after deleting the first: ${named()}`);
    if ((await m.value("dupa")) !== 2) throw new Error("dupa is not the second cell's value");
    return "dupa (2) became dupa, with value 2, once the first dupa was deleted";
  } finally { for (const v of [...runtime._variables]) if (v._module === m) v.delete(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_7 = spec_rejectsFor("S1.7")
// %%
test_spec_S1_8 = {
  const got = spec_okCells("const a = () => 1, b = (a: number) => a + 1;");
  if (got !== "a:ok,b:ok") throw new Error(got);
  const L = await spec_load("const a = () => 1, b = (a: number) => a + 1;", {});
  try { spec_is(await L.value("b"), 2); } finally { L.dispose(); }
  return "one statement, two cells; both run (b is 2)";
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_10 = {
  // the names are read from S1.10's own text; each, declared as a type, is rejected
  const statement = [...runtime._variables].filter((v) => v._module === spec_module).map((v) => String(v._definition)).find((d) => d.includes("**S1.10**"));
  const names = /own types, \\`([^`\\]+)\\`/.exec(statement)[1].split(" ");
  spec_is([...names].sort(), soundscript.builtinTypeNames());
  const bad = names.filter((n) => JSON.stringify(spec_rulesOf({ source: `type ${n} = { readonly n: number };` })) !== '["builtin-type-name"]');
  if (bad.length) throw new Error(`not rejected: ${bad.join(", ")}`);
  // the cells read the built-in: a user Array without @mutates does not let push through, a user Pool does not retype the capability
  spec_is(spec_rulesOf({ source: "interface Array<T> { push(x: T): number }\nconst xs = () => [1];\nconst w = (xs) => xs.push(1);" }), ["builtin-type-name", "mutating-method"]);
  spec_is(spec_rulesOf({ source: "type Pool = { readonly m: number };\nconst a = (pool: Pool) => pool.m;" }), ["builtin-type-name", "no-member"]);
  spec_is(spec_okCells("type ListenerOf = { readonly n: number };\nconst one = () => (l: ListenerOf): number => l.n;"), "one:ok");
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S1.10")}; the list is the checker's, all ${names.length} names rejected; the cells read the built-in; another name is accepted`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_9 = {
  const st = soundscript.check("const a = (b: number) => b + 1;\nconst b = (a: number) => a + 1;").cells.map((c) => `${c.name}:${c.status}`).join(",");
  spec_is(st, "a:rejected,b:rejected");
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S1.9")}; both cells of a two-cell cycle are rejected`;
}
// %%
md`## 2. Inputs and names

A cell's parameters name the cells it reads. A body can use its parameters, its locals, a fixed set of prelude functions and intrinsics, and the capabilities it takes as parameters. No other name reaches it.`
// %%
md`**S2.1** A cell's parameters are its inputs, taken by name. A parameter that names no cell of the module, foreign declaration, shell cell or capability (or, section 11, a variable in another language) is \`[inject-unknown]\`. Only the cell's own parameter list is its input list, so a cell whose value is a function is two arrows: \`const double = () => (n: number) => n * 2\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.2** An input is a plain identifier. A destructuring pattern, a default value or a rest parameter is \`[inject-pattern]\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.3** An input with no type annotation has the value type of the cell it names. An annotated input must accept that value type; otherwise it is \`[inject-type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.4** The value type of a cell is its return type \`T\`, except that \`Promise<T>\` gives \`T\`, a generator or async generator yielding \`Y\` gives \`Y\`, and \`StateCell<T>\` gives \`T\`. Annotating the input with the wrapped type, such as \`Promise<number>\`, is \`[inject-type]\`. The runtime awaits a cell's value, so since 2026-11-23 (a9) every level of \`Promise\` is taken off, in each member of a union: \`Promise<Promise<number>>\` gives \`number\`, and so does a cell that is not async and returns \`Promise.resolve(5)\`, a generator that yields a promise, and a state cell that holds one (before, these readers were typed \`Promise<…>\` and received the number). A promise inside a record or array is not awaited and keeps its type. A \`State<T>\` or \`Events<T>\` input keeps the promise: \`read\` and a listener's action get the promise itself.`
// %%
md`**S2.5** The capabilities are the inputs \`now: Clock\`, \`random: Random\`, \`fetch: Fetch\`, \`log: Log\`, \`invalidation: Invalidation\`, \`compile: Compile\` and \`pool: Pool\`. \`Clock\` and \`Random\` are \`() => number\`. Any other annotation on a capability, such as \`now: number\`, is \`[inject-type]\`. The value of \`pool\` is not part of the core: a module with a cell that takes \`pool\` imports \`ss_pool\` from the add-on \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-workers\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.6** A body may name its parameters, its own locals, the prelude functions \`state action mut freeze borrow snapshot equals on read messageOf memo cast\`, and the intrinsics \`Object Array Math JSON Number String Boolean BigInt Map Set Error TypeError RangeError RegExp Promise ArrayBuffer URL URLSearchParams Infinity NaN undefined isNaN isFinite parseInt parseFloat encodeURIComponent decodeURIComponent encodeURI decodeURI atob btoa structuredClone Float32Array Float64Array Int8Array Uint8Array Uint8ClampedArray Int16Array Uint16Array Int32Array Uint32Array\`. Any other free name is \`[free-name]\`: \`Date\`, \`console\`, \`window\`, \`globalThis\`, \`setTimeout\`, \`eval\`, \`Function\`. A call of \`structuredClone\` with an argument whose type is or may hold a function or a symbol, or is \`unknown\`, \`object\` or a type parameter, is \`[type]\`: the call would throw. A prelude function is only called: naming one as a value is \`[prelude-value]\`, except \`read\`, which is \`[read-outside-action]\` (S6.8), and \`on\`, which is \`[listener-placement]\` (S7.3). Declaring a local or a parameter of the same name is \`[prelude-shadow]\`. \`mut\`, \`freeze\` and \`borrow\` are names so that a call of one, or the name as a value, is \`[removed-call]\` (S4.2). Since 2026-11-24 (w1) a \`const\` or \`let\` of a block is in scope for the whole block, as in JavaScript: a read of its name before the declaration, in the block or in a function made there, names it and not a binding outside the block, and is \`[free-name]\` (before w1 such a read in a function resolved to the outer binding, so a local \`Math\` declared later changed what a read of \`Math.max\` was checked as). This is stricter than JavaScript: a function made in the block that reads a later \`const\` is \`[free-name]\` even when it is called only after the declaration, which JavaScript runs.`
// %%
md`**S2.7** Reading \`Math.random\`, \`Object.defineProperty\`, \`Object.defineProperties\`, \`Object.setPrototypeOf\`, or \`prototype\` on \`Object\` or \`Array\` is \`[banned-member]\`, also through a local bound to the intrinsic, through destructuring such as \`const { random } = Math\`, and through a computed key such as \`Math["random"]\`. A value with a banned member is not assignable to a type that names it, so passing \`Math\` where \`{ random: () => number }\` is expected is \`[type]\`. A \`__proto__\` key in an object literal, written as a name, a string or a computed literal, is \`[banned-member]\` too: the name and string forms set the prototype, and the computed and shorthand forms, which make an own property, are rejected so that no spelling of the key is accepted. \`prototype\` on any other value is rejected by another rule, usually \`[no-member]\`. Since 2026-11-24, on a value whose type has an index signature (\`{ [k: string]: T }\`, \`Record<string, T>\`), a computed member read or write, a \`delete\` or an \`in\` test whose key may be any string is \`[banned-member]\`: a key known only at run time can name an inherited member such as \`toString\` or \`__proto__\`. The key must be a string literal, a union of string literals (a \`const\` bound to a literal has its literal type) or a number. Entries with run-time keys go in a \`Map<string, T>\` (\`m.get(k)\`, \`m.set(k, v)\`, \`m.has(k)\`). \`Object.keys\`, \`Object.values\` and \`Object.entries\` read own properties only and are accepted; \`Object.fromEntries\` with a \`"__proto__"\` key makes an own property and leaves the prototype as it is. Since 2026-11-24 (w1) a member of a type literal, an alias or an interface, optional or required, and a key of an object literal, named after a member of \`Object.prototype\` (the names are read from \`Object.prototype\` when the checker loads: \`toString\`, \`valueOf\`, \`hasOwnProperty\`, \`isPrototypeOf\`, \`constructor\` and the rest) or \`toJSON\` or \`then\` is \`[banned-member]\`, and so is a member named \`__proto__\`: a template string, \`+\`, \`==\`, \`String()\`, \`join\`, \`sort\`, \`JSON.stringify\` and \`await\` call a value's own member of such a name, so a conversion would run code of the program's (from t1 until w1 only an optional member was refused). A type or an interface that declares such a member is \`[banned-member]\` once, for the module, and is not loaded, so a cell that uses it is \`[unknown-type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.8** A body that uses a cell by its name instead of taking it as a parameter is \`[cell-by-name]\`. That includes a cell calling itself. A local with a cell's name is a local.`
// %%
md`**S2.9** A body is type-checked with \`null\` and \`undefined\` kept apart from other types. A mismatch is \`[type]\`, except for five checks with rules of their own: a parameter of a local function with neither an annotation nor a type from where the function is passed is \`[untyped-parameter]\`; a call with too few or too many arguments is \`[arity]\`; a property that an object literal's declared type does not have is \`[excess-property]\`; a value that may be \`undefined\` or \`null\` used as a number, read from, indexed or called is \`[possibly-undefined]\`; an assignment to a \`const\` local is \`[const-assign]\`. From w1 until y1 (2026-11-24) a value given a type that did not declare a part of it was \`[forgets]\` unless that part was plain data; since y1 a record type may leave out any member: a response or iterator left out is refused at run time wherever the value is handed over, and a promise left out where it is in a cell's value whose type names no promise (S4.6), and a function left out is not reached by a conversion (S2.7). Since 2026-11-24 (w1) a value that is not a record (a function, an array, a primitive, an \`Error\`, a \`Map\`, a typed array and the like) is \`[forgets]\` when given a record type, \`{}\` and a record of optional members included. Since 2026-11-24 (w1) \`JSON.stringify(v)\` where \`v\` may be \`undefined\`, a function, \`unknown\`, \`object\` or a type parameter is \`[type]\`: \`JSON.stringify\` gives \`undefined\` for those, not a string; \`JSON.stringify(v ?? null)\` is accepted. A value whose own type holds a promise, a generator or an iterator (\`Iterable\`, what \`values()\` gives), given \`unknown\` or \`object\`, is \`[forgets]\`: the checker reports this direct case, and one that reaches \`unknown\` another way (a join with a value typed \`unknown\`, a type parameter) is refused at run time where it is handed over (S4.6). From x1 until y1 (2026-11-24) any value that was not plain data was \`[forgets]\` there, a function and a \`Mut\` included, with the arguments of built-ins that keep nothing exempt; since y1 a function or a \`Mut\` given \`unknown\` or \`object\` is accepted. An \`Action\` given \`unknown\` or \`object\` itself is \`[action-pass]\` (S6.6), not \`[forgets]\`. A function result typed \`void\` takes \`undefined\` only: a callback that returns a value where \`(x: T) => void\` is declared is \`[type]\` (a block body that returns nothing is accepted), and \`forEach\` declares its callback's result \`unknown\`. A function given a type with a call signature that declares a member every function has (\`name\`, \`length\`, \`bind\` and the others of \`Function.prototype\`, read when the checker runs) is \`[forgets]\` unless the function's own type declares it. \`Object.fromEntries\` gives a record whose keys come from run-time data, so one may be \`then\`, \`toJSON\` or \`toString\`, which the run time calls: a call whose values are not plain data is \`[type]\`, and since y1 values typed \`unknown\` or \`object\` count as not plain (one may be an \`Action\` a join made \`unknown\`). Check time and run time: the rules of this statement are the checker's; a value that a type wrongly describes as plain data reaches no run-time check.`
// %%
md`**S2.10** \`viewof\`, \`mutable\` and \`$\` followed by digits (\`$0\`, \`$12\`) are reserved. Using one as the name of a cell, a parameter or a local is \`[reserved-name]\`, and so is \`$0\` as a property key or a member name. \`viewof\` and \`mutable\` may still be a property key or a member name, and a type may use any of them. A compiled cell is Observable JavaScript, which reads \`viewof\` and \`mutable\` as keywords and names a cell's \`viewof\` inputs \`$0\`, \`$1\` and so on. Since 2026-11-23 a name that starts with \`ss_\` or \`$ss$\` is reserved in the same places: the compiler names the runtime cells a compiled cell takes \`ss_<name>\`, inserts calls of \`ss_closure\` and \`ss_methods\` with a list whose parameter is \`ss_i\`, and names a duplicate or a type \`$ss$…\`; a binding of such a name would replace what it inserted. A cell may not be named \`Generators\` or \`invalidation\`, which compiled cells take from the module; a parameter named \`invalidation\` is the capability (S2.5). Since 2026-11-24 a cell may not take the name of a prelude function or \`h\` (the list is the toolchain's, \`tcPrelude\`: \`state\`, \`action\`, \`snapshot\`, \`equals\`, \`on\`, \`read\`, \`messageOf\`, \`memo\`, \`cast\` and \`h\`): a compiled cell that uses one binds it from the runtime module, so a cell of that name clashes with that binding. The runtime module, which defines them, is exempt; the checker knows it by the identity of the module it is given, not by the names of its cells. A parameter or a local may still take such a name. A BigInt literal as a property key (\`{ 1n() {} }\`) is unsupported. Since 2026-11-23 (a9) a cell whose text contains \`/*ss\` or \`/*{lang:"ss"}*/\`, in a string, a comment or a type, is \`[reserved-name]\`: the compiler marks what it inserts with that text and refuses such a cell, which the checker accepted before. In a string, write it split: \`"/" + "*ss"\`. Since 2026-11-24 (t1) a cell may not take the name of a capability or of a global that a compiled cell takes by name from outside the module: each capability (\`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\`, \`log\`, \`invalidation\`, \`compile\`, \`pool\`, the compiler's \`tcCaps\`), each global the compiler passes as an input (\`structuredClone\`, \`URLSearchParams\`) and each global the checker declares (\`Map\`, \`Set\`, \`URL\`, \`JSON\`, \`Math\`, \`Object\`, \`parseInt\` and the rest of \`ALLOWED_GLOBALS\`); such a name is \`[reserved-name]\`. A compiled cell that reads such a name reads the capability or the global, not the cell, while the module run as one script reads the cell, so the two runners gave different values. A cell name that holds \`$\` is \`[reserved-name]\`: the runners name a state cell's handle \`x$ref\`. In a cell that holds JSX, a binding named \`h\` (a local, a parameter, a function name or a \`catch\` parameter, in any pattern) is \`[reserved-name]\`: each element is a call \`h(...)\`, which would call the binding. Since 2026-11-24 (w1) a binding (a cell, a local, a parameter, a function name or a \`catch\` parameter) whose name the parser reads, alone as an expression, as something other than a name is \`[reserved-name]\`: the TypeScript parser takes \`const this = …\`, which JavaScript does not parse. The check asks the parser, so it covers each keyword it knows; a contextual keyword that JavaScript takes as a name, such as \`async\` or \`of\`, is accepted.`
// %%
md`**S2.11** The return type of an async function is \`Promise<T>\`, and of a generator function, sync or async, \`Generator<Y>\`; any other annotation is \`[type]\`. Each \`yield\` of a generator annotated \`Generator<Y>\` gives a \`Y\`, and a value of another type is \`[type]\`. A promise that an async function returns is unwrapped at run time, so the checker types the return as what the promise resolves to, as \`await\` would (since 2026-11-23; it was \`[type]\`). The result of \`then\` and of \`Promise.resolve\` is typed the same way: a callback that returns a \`Promise<T>\` gives a \`Promise<T>\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.12** \`messageOf(e)\` is the text of a caught value \`e\`: the \`message\` of an \`Error\` when it is a string, otherwise \`String(e)\`. It does not throw: when reading the message or making the string throws, it is \`(a thrown value whose text cannot be read)\`. A call with other than one argument is \`[arity]\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.13** \`memo(f)\` is a function of the type of \`f\`. For an argument list it has seen, it gives the result it stored for that list. A key is the number of arguments and each argument in turn: a primitive by \`Object.is\`, so \`0\` and \`-0\` are two keys, and any other value by identity. A call that misses deep-freezes its arguments in place and calls \`f\` with them, as a call of an action does. A caller that writes such an argument after the call gets a \`TypeError\` (S4.6). The result is deep-frozen and stored. A call with an argument that freezing replaces by a copy, such as a typed array or a \`Map\` made by JavaScript, is not stored. Nor is a call whose arguments reach a function at any depth, such as a record with a function field passed to a parameter \`{ readonly n: number }\` by width; an object of a kind the walk does not know counts as reaching one. Such a call is not one that S4.12 checks: nothing is stored for it. Such a function may read a \`Mut\` through a read-only name (S4.10) that the caller writes later, and a later call with the same key would be given what the first call stored. A call of \`f\` that throws stores nothing. Calling an action, writing a state cell or calling \`read\` while \`f\` computes a result throws. \`f\` is a function literal; \`memo\` of any other value is unsupported. In \`const fib = memo((n: number): number => …)\`, \`fib\` is bound before the call is checked, so \`f\` may call \`fib\` when its parameters and its result are annotated. Without those annotations the cell is unsupported. A call with other than one argument is \`[arity]\`. A \`Mut\` local of a function, named in an argument of a call there of a memoised function that the cell binds with \`const f = memo(…)\`, is not written after that call: such a write is \`[mutating-method]\` or \`[member-write]\`. A write in a later turn of a loop around the call, and a call of a helper that writes the local, are after it. A name inside \`snapshot(…)\` in the argument is not given to the function, since \`snapshot\` makes a copy. The checker does not see a write through a second name for the local, nor a call of a memoised function that the cell takes as an input; such a write throws at run time.`
// %%
md`**S2.14** \`f\` in \`memo(f)\` computes its result from its arguments only. It is \`[memo-pure]\` when \`f\` is async or a generator. It is \`[memo-pure]\` when a parameter type of \`f\` may be or hold a function, since a function may read a capability. It is \`[memo-pure]\` when a parameter type of \`f\` holds a \`Mut\`, \`State\`, \`StateCell\`, \`Action\`, \`Events\`, \`Listener\`, \`Promise\`, \`Generator\`, \`Elem\`, \`FetchResponse\`, \`URLSearchParams\` or \`DataView\`. It is \`[memo-pure]\` when the result type of \`f\` holds a \`Mut\`, a state type, a \`Promise\` or a \`Generator\`, or may hold an iterator by the test of S3.19, such as what \`[n].values()\` gives. A name that \`f\` uses from outside itself follows the rule for an action's body (S6.9): an input that depends on state, a capability, a parameter of an enclosing function, or a const computed from one of these is \`[memo-pure]\`. So is a const bound to a \`Mut\` local, such as \`c\` in \`const c: readonly number[] = xs\`, though S4.10 lets other functions capture it: the cell may write \`xs\` after a result is stored. A capability, \`log\` and \`invalidation\` included, and a value that holds a \`State\`, \`Action\`, \`Events\` or \`Listener\` are \`[memo-pure]\` as well. A \`let\` or a \`Mut\` that \`f\` captures is \`[let-capture]\` or \`[mut-capture]\` (S4.10). The memoised function may be kept, returned and placed in a record, as a function value may.`
// %%
