// %%
md`## 6. State cells and actions

A state cell is a cell that holds state. Only an action writes to a state cell. An action is called by an event handler, a listener or another action, never while a cell is being evaluated.`
// %%
md`**S6.1** A state cell is a sync, non-generator cell whose one returned expression is \`state(v)\`; its value type is the type of \`v\` (S2.4). \`state(...)\` anywhere else, bound to a local, in a conditional, in one of two returns, or in an async or generator cell, is \`[state-placement]\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.2** A state cell with inputs is made again, from its initial value, when an input changes. Until then it keeps what was written to it, also when it runs again with the same inputs. An input changes when it is not the same value (\`Object.is\`): a new object with equal content is a change, so a state cell whose input is an object built by another cell starts again each time that cell runs.`
// %%
md`**S6.3** An input that names a state cell, \`c\` or \`c: T\`, gets its value and runs again after each commit that wrote it (S6.14). An input \`c: State<T>\` gets the state cell and does not run again when it is written. \`State<T>\` on a cell that is not a state cell holding \`T\`, or on a capability or foreign declaration, is \`[inject-state]\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.4** A \`State<T>\` has \`set(v: T)\` and \`update(f: (v: T) => T)\` and no other member. Reading another member is \`[no-member]\`; a value of the wrong type is \`[type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.5** \`set\` and \`update\` outside the body of an \`action(...)\` are \`[state-write]\`, including in a function the cell returns and in a generator. At run time a write made while no action's function is running throws.`
// %%
md`**S6.6** \`action(f)\` has type \`Action<P>\`, where \`P\` is the parameter types of \`f\`. Calling an \`Action\` outside the body of an action is \`[action-call]\`. An \`Action\` may be returned, placed in a record or an array, or passed to a parameter typed \`Action\`. Giving it a type that does not say it is an \`Action\` is \`[action-pass]\`: \`unknown\`, \`object\`, a type parameter of a function of the module, or a record, array, tuple, union or function type that has one of these where the \`Action\` is. That holds for an argument, a spread element, an input, a default value, an annotated local, an assignment, a returned value and a thrown one. A type parameter of an intrinsic, such as the \`U\` of \`map\` or the \`T\` of \`Promise.resolve\`, takes the \`Action\`'s own type and is accepted. Since 2026-11-24 (t1) a record type with no required member and no index or call signature (\`{}\`, an empty interface, \`{ readonly a?: number }\`) hides as \`object\` does: it takes any object, so an \`Action\` given that type is \`[action-pass]\`. A record given that type keeps the members the type declares, and only the others are hidden. From w1 until y1 (2026-11-24) an \`Action\` held in a part of a value that the type it is given did not declare was \`[forgets]\` (S2.9); since y1 it is accepted: no member of the type reaches it, no conversion calls it (S2.7), and a call of it while a cell computes throws (the guard). Check time: \`[action-call]\` and \`[action-pass]\` for the direct cases; run time: the guard, and an \`Action\` call gives back \`undefined\` (S6.13). Since 2026-11-24 (w1) a compiled cell's body, and each step of a generator cell, runs under the run-time guard \`runCells\` puts around a cell: an action called while a cell computes, by a function the checker cannot see into, throws \`action called during a cell evaluation\` in both runners; before w1 the compiled cell ran it. Since 2026-11-24 (x1) the guard covers the whole evaluation: an async function written in a SoundScript cell and called under it is under it again after each \`await\`, an async generator's body after each \`await\` and \`yield\`, and a callback given to \`then\` or \`finally\` while a cell computes is under it whenever it runs, after the cell has finished too; an action called there throws the same error. An async function a cell keeps and an event handler or a listener calls is not under it, and nor is an async function from a module in another language after its first \`await\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.7** The content type of a state cell may not contain \`State\` or \`StateCell\`: not directly, and not in a record, an array, a union, a type argument or a function signature. That is \`[state-in-state]\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.8** Inside an action, \`read(r)\` with \`r: State<T>\` is a \`T\`: the stored value with every write made earlier in the call order, including by an action called before it in the same call. \`read\` outside the body of an action, or used other than by calling it, is \`[read-outside-action]\`, and at run time, while no action's function is running, it throws.`
// %%
md`**S6.9** The body of an action may not use a value that depends on state: an input that takes a state cell or an events cell by value, a capability other than \`invalidation\` and \`log\`, an input that takes a cell which depends on one of these, or a local computed from any of them. That is \`[action-capture]\`. Handles (\`State\`, \`Events\`), \`Action\`s, cells with no state, and the action's own parameters and locals are accepted. A value from the clock or the random source reaches an action as an argument.`
// %%
md`**S6.10** A write is applied to its call when it is made, so an \`update\` whose function throws throws out of the call that made it. A top-level call that throws keeps none of its writes, nor those of the actions it called; the writes of earlier calls are kept, and calls apply in the order they were made. An action called inside a call is a plain function call: when it throws and its caller catches the throw, the writes it made before throwing are kept.`
// %%
md`**S6.11** A state cell is two runtime variables: \`viewof c\` holds its handle and \`c\` holds its value. An input \`c: State<T>\` is an input on \`viewof c\` and gets the handle. The handle is the same object when the state cell is made again (S6.2), so a \`State<T>\` taken before writes to the new state cell. After the state cell was in error it may have a new handle (S8.5); a cell that takes its \`State<T>\` runs again with the handle it then has. While the state cell is in error, a cell that takes its \`State<T>\` does not run: it is blocked when the state cell does not check (S8.2), and has the error of the input that threw when the state cell or one of its inputs threw (S8.3). When the state cell's variables are deleted, the holder is rejected, \`[inject-unknown]\` (S2.1), once the module has seen the deletion; until then it raises the runtime's \`viewof c is not defined\`. A function made from the \`State<T>\` before an error and kept outside the dataflow may write to the old handle, which no cell reads.`
// %%
md`**S6.12** The function given to \`action(...)\` is synchronous. An async function, a generator, a function whose return type is or contains a promise-like, and an \`await\` anywhere in its body are \`[action-async]\`. To wait for something, an async cell waits, and a listener gives the result to an action, as in this section's fetch-then-store example.`
// %%
spec_s6_fetchThenStore = `const asked = (): StateCell<number> => state(0);
const ask = (asked: State<number>) => action(() => asked.update((n) => n + 1));
// the waiting: an async cell, run again for each request
const loaded = async (fetch: Fetch, asked: number) => {
  const r = await fetch(\`https://example.invalid/greeting?n=\${asked}\`);
  return r.ok ? await r.text() : null;
};
// each answer once, as an event
const arrived = function* (loaded: string | null) {
  if (loaded !== null) yield loaded;
};
const greeting = (): StateCell<string> => state("");
const store = (greeting: State<string>) => action((text: string) => greeting.set(text));
const onArrived = (arrived: Events<string>, store: Action<[string]>) => on(arrived, store);`
// %%
md`Fetch, then store. \`ask\` writes a request; \`loaded\` is an async cell that takes it, waits for the answer, and gives the text; \`arrived\` yields that text once; \`onArrived\` gives it to \`store\`, a synchronous action.

\`\`\`ts
${spec_s6_fetchThenStore}
\`\`\``
// %%
spec_s6_latestWins = `type Answer = { readonly n: number; readonly text: string | null };
const asked = (): StateCell<number> => state(0);
const ask = (asked: State<number>) => action(() => asked.update((n) => n + 1));
// the answer carries the request it answers
const loaded = async (fetch: Fetch, asked: number): Promise<Answer> => {
  const r = await fetch(\`https://example.invalid/greeting?n=\${asked}\`);
  return { n: asked, text: r.ok ? await r.text() : null };
};
const arrived = function* (loaded: Answer) {
  yield loaded;
};
const greeting = (): StateCell<string> => state("");
// kept only if it answers the latest request
const store = (greeting: State<string>, asked: State<number>) =>
  action((a: Answer) => {
    if (a.n === read(asked) && a.text !== null) greeting.set(a.text);
  });
const onArrived = (arrived: Events<Answer>, store: Action<[Answer]>) => on(arrived, store);`
// %%
md`Latest wins. An answer to an earlier request still reaches \`store\`, which compares the request it answers with \`read(asked)\` and drops it.

\`\`\`ts
${spec_s6_latestWins}
\`\`\``
// %%
spec_s6_everyAnswer = `type Answer = { readonly n: number; readonly text: string | null };
// the request in flight, and those asked for meanwhile
const current = (): StateCell<number | null> => state<number | null>(0);
const queue = (): StateCell<number[]> => state<number[]>([]);
const issued = (): StateCell<number> => state(1);
const ask = (current: State<number | null>, queue: State<number[]>, issued: State<number>) =>
  action(() => {
    const n = read(issued);
    issued.set(n + 1);
    if (read(current) === null) current.set(n);
    else queue.update((q) => [...q, n]);
  });
// reads only current, so its input does not change while it waits
const loaded = async (fetch: Fetch, current: number | null): Promise<Answer | null> => {
  if (current === null) return null;
  try {
    const r = await fetch(\`https://example.invalid/greeting?n=\${current}\`);
    return { n: current, text: r.ok ? await r.text() : null };
  } catch (e) {
    return { n: current, text: null };
  }
};
const arrived = function* (loaded: Answer | null) {
  if (loaded !== null) yield loaded;
};
const greeting = (): StateCell<string[]> => state<string[]>([]);
// records the answer and starts the next request
const store = (greeting: State<string[]>, current: State<number | null>, queue: State<number[]>) =>
  action((a: Answer) => {
    if (a.n !== read(current)) return;
    greeting.update((g) => [...g, a.text ?? "(failed)"]);
    const q = read(queue);
    const head = q[0];
    if (head !== undefined) {
      current.set(head);
      queue.set(q.slice(1));
    } else current.set(null);
  });
const onArrived = (arrived: Events<Answer>, store: Action<[Answer]>) => on(arrived, store);`
// %%
md`Every answer, in order. One request is in flight at a time; an ask made meanwhile waits in \`queue\`, and \`store\` starts the next one. A failed fetch is recorded as "(failed)" and the queue goes on.

\`\`\`ts
${spec_s6_everyAnswer}
\`\`\``
// %%
md`**S6.13** An action call made while no action's function is running, by an event handler, a listener delivering a value or JavaScript, is a top-level call. It runs to completion before its caller goes on. An action called while an action's function is running is part of that call. A call of an \`Action\` is typed \`void\` and gives back \`undefined\`, whatever its function returns (since 2026-11-24, y1; before, a call inside an action gave back its function's value).`
// %%
md`**S6.14** The writes of a top-level call, including those of the actions it calls, commit together once, after the call returns. Calls that return before that commit share it, so a reader runs once for them and sees none of the values between. A call that throws keeps none of them (S6.10). Inside the call, \`read(r)\` is the committed value with the call's own earlier writes (S6.8).`
// %%
md`S6.15 Removed 2026-11-20: actions are synchronous (S6.12).`
// %%
md`**S6.16** A throw from a top-level call goes to its caller.`
// %%
md`S6.17 Removed 2026-11-20: actions are synchronous (S6.12).`
// %%
md`**S6.18** The content type of a state cell may not admit \`undefined\`: it may not be \`undefined\`, \`void\` or \`unknown\`, or a union with one of them, directly or through a type alias. That is \`[state-undefined]\`. A property of the content may be optional. A state cell's value variable is Observable's \`Generators.input\` of its handle, which skips an \`undefined\` value, so the cell would have none; use \`null\`. \`State\`, \`StateCell\` and \`Events\` written without a type argument are \`[type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.19** A function made in the body of an action writes, reads and calls \`Action\`s only where it runs before the action's function returns. It may be called in the body, directly or through a local function that is only called, or passed to an array method that calls it before returning, such as \`forEach\` or \`map\`, or to \`update\`. Returned, stored, placed in a record, an array or a state cell, given to \`.then\`, to a function of the module or to an intrinsic that keeps it, such as \`Map.set\`, or made async or a generator, it may run after the call, and its \`set\`, \`update\`, \`read\` and \`Action\` calls are \`[state-write]\`, \`[read-outside-action]\` and \`[action-call]\` as in S6.5, S6.8 and S6.6. A \`set\` or \`update\` taken as a value is a function that writes and is held to the same rule.`
// %%
md`**S6.20** A \`key\` attribute on a component's element is the renderer's, as on an intrinsic element: it is a \`string | number\` and is not checked against the component's props (since 2026-11-23; it was \`[excess-property]\`). The component is not given \`key\`: \`h\` leaves it out of the props, as React does (since 2026-11-23, a9; the component received it before, so a props type that declared \`key\` read a value it was never checked against). A component whose props type declares \`key\` is \`[type]\`; name the prop something else, such as \`id\`. An \`Elem\` is made only by JSX. A record with the fields of an element is not an \`Elem\`, and giving one where an \`Elem\` or a JSX child is expected, or returning one from a component, is \`[type]\`. The renderer draws only elements JSX made: any other value, a record of the same shape included, is drawn as text: a primitive as \`String\` gives it, an object as \`[object Object]\`, \`[object Array]\` or \`[object Function]\`. No function in it is called, its own \`toString\`, \`valueOf\` and event props included.`
// %%
md`The cells of this section. \`s6_copy\` reads \`s6_count\` when it is called; \`s6_bumpThenCopy\` writes \`s6_count\` and calls \`s6_copy\`, which sees that write (S6.8). \`s6_controls\` holds two actions in a record. \`s6_view\` has a button for each of \`s6_increment\` and \`s6_bumpThenCopy\` and shows both counts.`
// %%
//ss: ./spec-6.tsx
// %%
